{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Treat node1 `/etc/inventory/app.conf` as the source of truth and maintain node2 as its one-way synchronized copy.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor created the initially absent source on node1, installed a one-way node1-to-node2 synchronization mechanism, changed the source, and directly observed matching contents and SHA-256 hashes on both nodes. Matching files were observed again after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3e3639c7969644d3","cmd-85401111145a4f6a","cmd-133e3598248a44ec","cmd-7c257411c8a141f0","cmd-266d517fd66d40bf","cmd-5f054572f51b49b5","cmd-54737f5dc6f34a82"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Automatically pick up node1 configuration changes on node2 within 15 minutes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A changed source was successfully transferred and verified within seconds. The persistent, enabled timer has a 10-minute interval with 30-second accuracy, remained active after restart, and journal evidence directly records successful scheduled synchronization executions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-133e3598248a44ec","cmd-7c257411c8a141f0","cmd-266d517fd66d40bf","cmd-0fdf8e79a66f47bb","cmd-3493910c6d5c4048","cmd-50397818028542ae"]}],"confidence":0.94,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Source-file and credential creation (`cmd-3e3639c7969644d3`), node2 authorization and destination preparation (`cmd-fead45095cfa4b73`), host-key pinning (`cmd-b9a1d1714348491d`), and service/timer installation (`cmd-85401111145a4f6a`) directly established the synchronization solution. The retained marker introduced by `cmd-133e3598248a44ec` provided the bounded propagation test and documents source management. Manual service starts were bounded verification, while both reboot mutations were required by the provider-wide evidence protocol. The reboot log left no observed residue. Final lifecycle checks show no failed services, package damage, or unexpected temporary files on either node.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3e3639c7969644d3","cmd-fead45095cfa4b73","cmd-b9a1d1714348491d","cmd-85401111145a4f6a","cmd-133e3598248a44ec","cmd-575c944661064a55","cmd-33054d4e30284e0e","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 is demonstrably the one-way source, node2 holds the matching replica, and an enabled persistent 10-minute timer successfully invokes the synchronization service. The mandatory restart protocol is strong for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, acknowledgement, observed outage, changed boot identity, and recovered SSH, relevant service, credentials, and synchronized data. Fixed after-executor boot identities independently agree with the executor's post-restart observations.","limitations":["The changed-content test explicitly invoked the synchronization service; the separate scheduled timer executions succeeded but did not carry a newly introduced test change. Thus the 15-minute conclusion combines direct transfer behavior with direct scheduler execution and configuration evidence rather than one unattended end-to-end timed mutation.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory the installed synchronization files, systemd units, or SSH authorization, so those final details rely on the executor command observations."]}
